WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.720
As soon as you start living your M.V.P. and pursuing your 90-Day Way goals, I can promise you

00:07.720 --> 00:09.280
one thing.

00:09.280 --> 00:11.440
You will face resistance.

00:11.440 --> 00:13.440
It's inevitable.

00:13.440 --> 00:16.400
Resistance isn't a sign that you're on the wrong path.

00:16.400 --> 00:19.600
It's proof that you're moving forward.

00:19.600 --> 00:26.480
Often, that resistance is in the friction created in the gap between others' expectations

00:26.480 --> 00:29.140
and your own intentions.

00:29.140 --> 00:35.500
How you lead your team through that gap is a defining moment of leadership.

00:35.500 --> 00:37.620
Athletes don't see this as a roadblock.

00:37.620 --> 00:45.760
They see it as the weight in their leadership weight training, building mental muscle memory.

00:45.760 --> 00:51.580
Every time you push against your core, your purpose, you get stronger.

00:51.580 --> 00:54.540
This resistance comes in two forms.

00:54.540 --> 00:57.740
First, there is internal resistance.

00:57.740 --> 01:04.140
This is the voice of self-doubt, the imposter syndrome that whispers, who are you to do

01:04.140 --> 01:05.140
this?

01:05.140 --> 01:11.980
It's the comfort of your old habits fighting against the discomfort of the new ambitions.

01:11.980 --> 01:18.420
The key to overcoming this is to acknowledge the feelings without giving it authority.

01:18.420 --> 01:20.640
Your playbook has a drill on this.

01:20.640 --> 01:25.980
You will name the voice of your inner critic and you will arm yourself with a counter-statement

01:25.980 --> 01:28.420
rooted in your purpose.

01:28.420 --> 01:31.900
Then, there is the external resistance.

01:31.900 --> 01:38.740
This comes from skeptical colleagues, limited resources, or organizational inertia.

01:38.740 --> 01:43.380
When you try to change things, the system often pushes back.

01:43.380 --> 01:47.460
This is where resilience muscle is critical.

01:47.460 --> 01:52.740
One of the most powerful resilience tactics here is reframing.

01:52.740 --> 01:55.060
Let me make this personal.

01:55.100 --> 02:01.780
Early in my career, I pitched a new initiative and the division leader told me no.

02:01.780 --> 02:07.860
It would have been easy for me to see it as an obstacle and their no as a final rejection.

02:07.860 --> 02:10.940
But instead, I chose to reframe it.

02:10.940 --> 02:18.340
I reframed that skeptical executive not as a roadblock, but as a stress test for my idea.

02:18.340 --> 02:24.660
Their tough questions forced me to make a proposal that was stronger and bulletproof.

02:24.660 --> 02:31.380
I reframed their no not as rejection, but as a not yet, an opportunity to gather more

02:31.380 --> 02:35.060
data and refine my approach.

02:35.060 --> 02:40.780
That mental shift from obstacle to opportunity is the core athlete's skill.

